Bonus: GitLab on Kubernetes

  Step 1 — Assess your resources

  GitLab is resource-hungry (~4GB RAM minimum). Before anything, check what your
  machine/VM can provide and decide whether you'll deploy the full chart or a
  minimal configuration. This decision will shape everything else.

  Step 2 — Plan your network topology

  Your current k3d cluster only exposes port 80. GitLab will need its own
  ingress entry point. Think about:
  - Does your cluster need additional port mappings at creation time?
  - What hostname will GitLab be reachable at (e.g. gitlab.local)?
  - How will pods inside the cluster reach that hostname? (DNS resolution is the
  key problem here)

  Step 3 — Create the gitlab namespace

  Straightforward, but do it early — every resource you create will live there.

  Step 4 — Deploy GitLab with Helm

  Look up the official GitLab Helm chart (gitlab/gitlab). For a local setup
  you'll need to override several values:
  - Disable cert-manager and use a simpler TLS approach (or plain HTTP)
  - Set the global.hosts.domain to your chosen hostname
  - Tune down resource requests/limits so it actually fits your cluster
  - Disable cloud-specific components you don't need (Registry is optional,
  Runner is optional)

  This step requires the most iteration — GitLab has dozens of sub-charts.

  Step 5 — Make GitLab reachable from your host and from inside the cluster

  Two separate problems:
  - From your host browser → ingress + /etc/hosts entry
  - From ArgoCD pod inside the cluster → CoreDNS needs to resolve the same
  hostname to the right service

  Look into how k3d handles custom DNS resolution inside the cluster.

  Step 6 — Seed GitLab with your app content

  Retrieve the initial root password (it's in a Kubernetes secret). Then create
  a group, a project, and push the content that currently lives in your GitHub
  repo (Inheert/tclaereb) to your local GitLab.

  Step 7 — Reconfigure ArgoCD to use your GitLab repo

  ArgoCD needs to:
  1. Trust your GitLab instance (self-signed cert or HTTP — configure this in
  ArgoCD's repository settings)
  2. Have credentials if the repo is private
  3. Point my-app at the new GitLab repo URL instead of the GitHub one

  Step 8 — Update your bonus scripts

  Your run.sh currently hardcodes the GitHub URL. The bonus version should use your local
  GitLab URL instead. Also update install.sh to orchestrate the full setup: cluster
  creation, ArgoCD, and GitLab.

  Step 9 — Validate the full flow end-to-end

  The real test: push a change to your local GitLab, watch ArgoCD detect and sync it, verify
  the app deploys in the dev namespace. That proves the whole chain works.

  ---
  The main pitfalls to anticipate:
  - CoreDNS custom entries for your GitLab hostname (this trips most people up)
  - GitLab chart resource requests vs. available cluster capacity
  - ArgoCD trusting a self-signed or HTTP GitLab (check ArgoCD's
  --insecure-skip-server-verification equivalent for repos)

  Start with Step 2 — the network planning decision will determine whether you need to
  recreate the cluster.
